Article 14 An employee shall be identified as having a work-related injury under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Being injured in an accident during working hours and at the workplace due to work reasons;
(2) Being injured in an accident before or after working hours at the workplace while engaging in preparatory or finishing work related to work;
(3) Being injured by violence or other accidental injuries during working hours and at the workplace when performing work duties;
(4) Contracting an occupational disease;
(5) Being injured or missing due to an accident during a business trip due to work reasons;
(6) Being injured in a traffic accident for which the employee is not primarily responsible, or in an accident involving urban rail transit, passenger ferry, or train while commuting to or from work;
(7) Other circumstances that shall be identified as work-related injuries as provided by laws and administrative regulations.

Article 15 An employee shall be deemed to have a work-related injury under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Dying from a sudden illness during working hours and at the workplace, or dying within 48 hours after rescue efforts fail;
(2) Being injured in activities such as emergency rescue and disaster relief that safeguard national interests or public interests;
(3) An employee who previously served in the military and sustained a disability due to war or work-related injury, has obtained the Revolutionary Disabled Soldier Certificate, and suffers a relapse of the old injury after joining the employer.
An employee who falls under the circumstances of subparagraph (1) or (2) of the preceding paragraph shall enjoy work-related injury insurance benefits in accordance with the relevant provisions of these Regulations; an employee who falls under the circumstances of subparagraph (3) of the preceding paragraph shall enjoy work-related injury insurance benefits other than the lump-sum disability subsidy in accordance with the relevant provisions of these Regulations.

Article 16 Where an employee meets the provisions of Articles 14 and 15 of these Regulations but falls under any of the following circumstances, the injury or death shall not be identified as a work-related injury or deemed as a work-related injury:
(1) Intentional crime;
(2) Drunkenness or drug use;
(3) Self-inflicted injury or suicide.

Article 18 An application for work-related injury identification shall be accompanied by the following materials:
(1) The application form for work-related injury identification;
(2) Evidentiary materials proving the existence of an employment relationship (including de facto employment relationship) with the employer;
(3) A medical diagnosis certificate or an occupational disease diagnosis certificate (or an occupational disease diagnosis appraisal certificate).
The application form for work-related injury identification shall include basic information such as the time, place, and cause of the accident, as well as the degree of injury to the employee.
If the materials provided by the applicant for work-related injury identification are incomplete, the social insurance administrative department shall inform the applicant in writing at one time of all the materials that need to be supplemented. After the applicant supplements the materials as required in the written notice, the social insurance administrative department shall accept the application.

Article 35 Where an employee is assessed as having Grade 1 to Grade 4 disabilities due to a work-related injury, his labor relationship shall be retained, he shall withdraw from the work post, and shall enjoy the following benefits:
(1) A lump-sum disability subsidy shall be paid from the work-related injury insurance fund according to the disability grade, the standards being: 27 months of his own wage for Grade 1 disability, 25 months of his own wage for Grade 2 disability, 23 months of his own wage for Grade 3 disability, and 21 months of his own wage for Grade 4 disability;
(2) A monthly disability allowance shall be paid from the work-related injury insurance fund according to the disability grade, the standards being: 90% of his own wage for Grade 1 disability, 85% of his own wage for Grade 2 disability, 80% of his own wage for Grade 3 disability, and 75% of his own wage for Grade 4 disability. If the actual amount of the disability allowance is lower than the local minimum wage standard, the difference shall be made up by the work-related injury insurance fund;
(3) After the employee with work-related injury reaches the retirement age and goes through the retirement procedures, the disability allowance shall be discontinued, and he shall enjoy basic old-age insurance benefits in accordance with the relevant provisions of the State. If the basic old-age insurance benefits are lower than the disability allowance, the difference shall be made up by the work-related injury insurance fund.
Where an employee is assessed as having Grade 1 to Grade 4 disabilities due to a work-related injury, the employer and the employee individually shall pay the basic medical insurance premiums based on the disability allowance as the base.

Article 37 Where an employee is assessed as having Grade 7 to Grade 10 disabilities due to a work-related injury, he shall enjoy the following benefits:
(1) A lump-sum disability subsidy shall be paid from the work-related injury insurance fund according to the disability grade, the standards being: 13 months of his own wage for Grade 7 disability, 11 months of his own wage for Grade 8 disability, 9 months of his own wage for Grade 9 disability, and 7 months of his own wage for Grade 10 disability;
(2) Where a labor or employment contract expires and is terminated, or the employee himself proposes to dissolve the labor or employment contract, a lump-sum medical subsidy for work-related injury shall be paid from the work-related injury insurance fund, and a lump-sum employment subsidy for disability shall be paid by the employer. The specific standards for the lump-sum medical subsidy for work-related injury and the lump-sum employment subsidy for disability shall be prescribed by the people's governments of provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government.

Article 39 Where an employee dies due to a work-related injury, his close relatives shall receive from the work-related injury insurance fund a funeral subsidy, a pension for the dependents of the deceased, and a lump-sum subsidy for death due to work-related injury in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) The funeral subsidy shall be six months' average monthly wage of employees in the overall planning region in the preceding year;
(2) The pension for the dependents of the deceased shall be paid at a certain proportion of the employee's own wage to the relatives who were mainly supported by the employee who died due to work-related injury and who are incapable of working. The standards are: 40% per month for the spouse, 30% per month for each other relative, and an additional 10% per month on the basis of the said standards for each elderly person without spouse or each orphan. The total approved pensions for all dependents shall not exceed the wage of the employee before his death due to work-related injury. The specific scope of dependents shall be prescribed by the social insurance administrative department of the State Council;
(3) The lump-sum subsidy for death due to work-related injury shall be 20 times the national per capita disposable income of urban residents in the preceding year.
Where a disabled employee dies due to work-related injury during the period of suspension of work with pay, his close relatives shall enjoy the benefits provided in paragraph 1 of this Article.
Where an employee with Grade 1 to Grade 4 disabilities dies after the expiration of the period of suspension of work with pay, his close relatives may enjoy the benefits provided in subparagraphs (1) and (2) of paragraph 1 of this Article.

Article 42 Where a worker with work-related injury falls under any of the following circumstances, the worker shall cease to enjoy the work-related injury insurance benefits:
(1) losing the conditions for enjoying benefits;
(2) refusing to undergo labor capacity appraisal;
(3) refusing to receive medical treatment.

Article 46 The agency shall specifically handle work-related injury insurance affairs and perform the following duties:
(1) collecting work-related injury insurance premiums in accordance with the provisions of the people's governments of provinces, autonomous regions, or municipalities directly under the Central Government;
(2) verifying the total payroll and number of workers of the employer, handling the registration of work-related injury insurance, and being responsible for maintaining records of the employer's premium payments and the workers' enjoyment of work-related injury insurance benefits;
(3) conducting investigations and statistics on work-related injury insurance;
(4) managing the expenditure of the work-related injury insurance fund in accordance with the provisions;
(5) verifying and determining work-related injury insurance benefits in accordance with the provisions;
(6) providing free consulting services to workers with work-related injuries or their close relatives.

Article 55 In any of the following circumstances, the relevant entity or individual may, according to law, apply for administrative reconsideration or institute an administrative action before a People's Court:
(1) where the worker applying for work-related injury determination, or his close relative, or the worker's employer disagrees with the decision not to accept the application for work-related injury determination;
(2) where the worker applying for work-related injury determination, or his close relative, or the worker's employer disagrees with the conclusion of work-related injury determination;
(3) where the employer disagrees with the premium rate determined by the agency;
(4) where a medical institution or assistive device fitting institution that has concluded a service agreement considers that the agency has failed to perform the relevant agreement or provisions;
(5) where a worker with work-related injury or his close relative disputes the work-related injury insurance benefits verified by the agency.

Article 57 Where any staff member of the social insurance administrative department falls under any of the following circumstances, a sanction shall be imposed according to law; if the circumstances are serious and constitute a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) failing to accept an application for work-related injury determination without justifiable reasons, or fraudulently identifying a person who does not meet the conditions for work-related injury as a worker with work-related injury;
(2) failing to properly preserve the evidentiary materials for the application for work-related injury determination, resulting in the loss of the relevant evidence;
(3) accepting property from the party.

Article 58 Where the agency commits any of the following acts, the social insurance administrative department shall order it to make corrections, and impose disciplinary sanctions according to law on the directly responsible person in charge and other responsible persons; if the circumstances are serious and constitute a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law; if economic losses are caused to the party, the agency shall bear compensation liability according to law:
(1) failing to maintain records of the employer's premium payments and the workers' enjoyment of work-related injury insurance benefits in accordance with the provisions;
(2) failing to verify and determine work-related injury insurance benefits in accordance with the provisions;
(3) accepting property from the party.

Article 61 Where an organization or individual engaged in work capability assessment falls under any of the following circumstances, the social insurance administrative department shall order correction and impose a fine of not less than RMB 2,000 yuan but not more than RMB 10,000 yuan; if the circumstances are serious and constitute a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) providing false assessment opinions;
(2) providing false diagnostic certificates;
(3) accepting property from the parties.
